## 2. Mode Support

### 2.1 Definition

For each mode, compute the mode vector on the 64 sites:

$$v(x, y) = \cos\left(\frac{2\pi(n_1 x + n_2 y)}{8}\right)$$

The **support** of the mode is the number of sites where |v| exceeds a threshold:

$$\text{support}(n_1, n_2) = \#\{(x, y) : |v(x, y)| > 0.1\}$$

### 2.2 Computed supports

| (n₁, n₂) | Support |
|:---:|:---:|
| (0, 4) | 64 |
| (4, 0) | 64 |
| (1, 3) | 48 |
| (1, 5) | 48 |
| (3, 1) | 48 |
| (3, 7) | 48 |
| (5, 1) | 48 |
| (5, 7) | 48 |
| (7, 3) | 48 |
| (7, 5) | 48 |
| (2, 2) | 32 |
| (2, 6) | 32 |
| (6, 2) | 32 |
| (6, 6) | 32 |

### 2.3 The support takes exactly three values

**Support = 64, 48, 32.**

These values are robust: they are unchanged for thresholds from 0.001 to 0.5.

### 2.4 The support values are framework constants

$$64 = N = d \times 2^d$$
$$48 = V = (d-1) \times 2^d$$
$$32 = \frac{N}{2} = V - H$$

where d = 4, N = 64, V = 48, H = 16 are the framework constants.

**The 14 modes sort into three groups whose sizes are exactly the framework constants N, V, and N/2.**

